<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaHodaah [The Blessing of Thanksgiving] — Halachah 6<br>Part 4 (§§ 37–50 / Oaseyos לז–נ)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:24</span> (<i>Amtza'isa D'Alma</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 37 — <i>Notzair</i> [Guards] = <i>Ratzon</i> [Will]: The Inflaming of Truth Through Desire</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Notzair</span> [guards/preserves] is the letters of <span class="h">ratzon</span> [will] — for the essential thing is the will, as explained in our writings extensively. This is the essential illumination of the inflaming of truth in the heart: the truth should illuminate and inflame in his heart always, in gradation and measure, until his will is strong always for Hashem, His Torah, and His true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — this is the essential thing, as above. Therefore, when one reaches the aspect of <span class="h">Notzair Chesed</span> [the eighth of the thirteen <span class="h">midos</span> of mercy], no further open kindling is needed, as above — one cannot illuminate further in the open. The essential tikun is through the strong will, which shines even more through the inflaming of truth — the aspect of Yosef, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 38 — Yosef's True Dreams and Interpreting Others' Dreams: Sleep, Sadness, and Exile</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span> merited true and accurate dreams, and also knew how to interpret the dreams of others during their sleep. Sleep is one-sixtieth of death, and sadness is the side of death. The essential sleep comes from the wearying of the mind. The root of holy sleep (= the sleep of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>) derives from the pursuit and obstructer: the mind pursues to perceive, the obstructer obstructs until it settles, etc. — through which the nine chambers are fashioned, and one perceives the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light. Even though these nine chambers do not cling and are not known — from there all the holy mental faculties of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> derive, for they are the lights of the <span class="h">tzachtzachos</span> [brilliant splendors] — the root of all the <span class="h">midos</span>, from which all mental faculties descend through the <span class="h">tzimtzumim</span> and vessels the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> fashion through their good deeds.</div>

<div class="p">However, due to the wearying of the mind through the pursuit and obstructer, one must sleep to give the mind rest. Then specifically, during sleep, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> sorts even more from the Chambers of Exchange, his soul ascends — the mind rising to where it rises, perceiving then what it perceives. This is the aspect of what is explained above (ois kaf-daled) in <span class="h">Parshas Vayaitzai</span>: <span class="v">"he encountered the place... he lodged there... he dreamt"</span> etc.; see there. Therefore one must take care to fall asleep amidst words of Torah, in order to sleep amidst joy — so that one does not taste the taste of death. This is David's plea: <span class="v">"illuminate my eyes, lest I sleep the death."</span> And it was established for every person to say before sleep in <span class="h">Birchas HaMapil</span>: <span class="mx">"illuminate my eyes, lest I sleep the death"</span> — as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is connected to: <span class="v">"how long shall I set counsel in my soul, sorrow in my heart by day? How long shall my enemy be exalted over me?"</span> — followed by <span class="v">"look... illuminate my eyes, lest I sleep the death."</span> He cried: since exile overpowers so greatly — its essence being sorrow and sadness (<span class="v">"sorrow in my heart by day"</span>) — I plead: <span class="v">"illuminate my eyes, lest I sleep the death"</span> — that Hashem illuminate his eyes so he can gladden his soul, so his sleep will not be in the aspect of the side of death (= sorrow and sadness). The four times <span class="v">"how long"</span> David cried in this <span class="h">mizmor</span> correspond to the four exiles, as Rashi explains. The essence of all exiles is sadness. David cried and prayed that Israel be saved from sorrow and sadness — that their enemies not be exalted over them through this, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, that they not sleep the death.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore immediately follows: <span class="v">"but I trusted in Your kindness; my heart shall exult in Your salvation; I shall sing to Hashem"</span> etc. He strengthened himself and all the souls of Israel encompassed in him: he trusts in His kindness, and through this gladdens himself — <span class="v">"my heart shall exult in Your salvation."</span> This is the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> for every person: to strengthen his heart in great trust in Hashem's kindnesses — certainly He will not abandon him — until he rejoices and exults: <span class="v">"my heart shall exult in Your salvation."</span></div>

<div class="p">But those who do not merit this — their sleep is seized by the side of death according to their flaw in the mind, and their dreams are confused, etc. (holy Zohar). Every flaw of the mind and <span class="h">da'as</span> is due to the flaw of the Arranger and Settler — one does not merit the power of the Arranger and Settler of the mind as needed. If one had proper <span class="h">yishuv hada'as</span> [settled mind], certainly he would not pursue follies and unbecoming actions. As explained elsewhere (<span class="lr">LM I:10</span>): the essential distancing from Hashem is due to lacking <span class="h">yishuv hada'as</span>. And: the essential <span class="h">yishuv hada'as</span> is through joy, etc.; see there.</div>

<div class="p">One depends on the other: because one does not overpower with joy — at least being grateful for being saved, for not being made a gentile, for being separated from those who err in many aspects, etc. — through this the mind is in exile. The more the exile of the mind overpowers, the more sadness overpowers; and so on. From this comes all distancing from Hashem. The <span class="h">tikun</span> is the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef), who knows in the depth of his wisdom how to gladden Jewish souls wherever they are, as above. Therefore he could interpret dreams — he could sort the good from the bad in the Chambers of Exchange, knowing the dream's interpretation even for common people, whose confusion derives from the Chambers of Exchange. He knew how to sort all this, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 39 — Yosef Interprets Pharaoh's Dream: The Seven Fat and Lean Years</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Yosef knew how to interpret Pharaoh's dream, and through this emerged from exile and slavery. Pharaoh — king of Egypt, the first exile — derives from the sin of Adam HaRishon, when sadness was drawn (the side of death): <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it... until you return to the ground."</span> Pharaoh saw the seven lean cows swallowing the seven fat cows (and the ears of grain, etc.): <span class="v">"they came into their insides and it was not known they had come"</span> — sadness and sorrow overpowering the good, which is the aspect of satiation, blessing, and joy (as Rashi explains on <span class="v">"the meadows are clothed with flocks... they shout for joy, they also sing"</span> — a shout of joy). But the seven years of famine (= the opposite: wailing and sadness, as in Yoel's decree of famine — <span class="v">"weep and wail... wail over the wheat and barley"</span>) overpower so greatly that they want to swallow and conceal Israel's joy entirely: <span class="v">"it was not known they had come"</span> — the essential prolonging of exile, as above.</div>

<div class="p">The essential sadness is in the matter of money, livelihood, and wealth — where the core sadness resides: <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it."</span> Even one who has wealth constantly lacks more and worries more than the poor: <span class="mx">"the more possessions, the more worry"</span> — <span class="v">"it was not known they had come."</span> Egypt is the aspect of the craving for money, as explained elsewhere. The <span class="h">tikun</span> is Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, who knows how to draw joy even in the depth of exile, interpreting the dream so we have sustenance: <span class="v">"G-d sent me before you for sustenance, to preserve for you a remnant on earth."</span> All the events between Yosef and Pharaoh, between Yosef and his brothers, etc. — all allude to the length of this exile until the wondrous end, as is known.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it says of Yosef: <span class="v">"can there be found such a man, in whom the spirit of G-d resides?"</span> — who knows how to blow and fan the dust from the Jewish heart, as above. <span class="v">"After G-d has made all this known to you, there is none as understanding and wise as you"</span> — since you can interpret such a dream and rectify it (because you can sort the Chambers of Exchange through overpowering with joy through your deep counsel). As stated in the Midrash (Parshah 86) on <span class="v">"he was a successful man"</span>: the word implies leaping and dancing — even in his slavery in the prison he leaped and succeeded in gladdening his soul. The essential counsel: to gladden oneself with foolish things, as explained in our writings extensively. Therefore Pharaoh said: <span class="v">"after G-d has made all this known to you"</span> — certainly <span class="v">"there is none as understanding and wise as you"</span> — for you certainly merit the blessing of intellect attained through sorting the Chambers of Exchange through overpowering with joy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 40 — "Only the Throne Shall I Make Greater": The Obstructer Above Even the <i>Tzadik</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You shall be over my house, and by your word all my people shall be sustained; only the throne shall I make greater than you."</span> The <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef), who merits ascending from the Chambers of Exchange through all the above ascents, merits all the above blessings and draws all bounty — physical and spiritual — to all the world: <span class="v">"you shall be over my house; by your word all my people shall be sustained"</span> — all bounty drawn through you. <span class="v">"Only the throne shall I make greater than you"</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">kisa d'miskasia</span> [the concealed throne], <span class="h">Keser</span>, the obstructer: "I shall be greater" — that your thought not pursue excessively; may you merit the Settler and Arranger, who obstructs the mind — <span class="h">Keser</span>, the concealed throne, as explained (<span class="lr">LM I:6</span>).</div>

<div class="p">This is also: <span class="v">"I am Pharaoh; without you no man shall raise his hand or foot"</span> — raising hands and feet, the totality of all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>: raising the feet to walk and arouse all worlds to Hashem, drawing blessing (= the hands). But nonetheless — <span class="v">"I am Pharaoh."</span> This parallels <span class="v">"only the throne shall I make greater,"</span> as Rashi explains there.</div>

<div class="p">All this alludes to what the <span class="h">Torah</span> above explains: one who merits sorting the Chambers of Exchange, ascending, etc. — attaining the blessing of intellect, the mind pursuing to perceive — his essential <span class="h">tikun</span> is through the obstructer (= <span class="h">Keser</span>, <span class="v">"only the throne"</span>). This itself is <span class="v">"I am Pharaoh,"</span> as above. The Midrash (Parshah 90) states: <span class="mx">"Raish Lakish said: two passages Moshe wrote us in the Torah, and we learn them from wicked Pharaoh. One verse says: 'you shall be only above' — could it mean like Me? The verse teaches: 'only' — My greatness is above yours. And we learn this from Pharaoh: 'you shall be...' — could it mean like me? 'Only' — my greatness is above yours. Likewise: 'you shall be holy' — like Me? 'For I am holy, Hashem your G-d' — My holiness is above your holiness. We learn this from Pharaoh: 'I am Pharaoh' — my greatness is above yours."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is clear to one who understands and examines the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: even though the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef) merited sorting as he merited until attaining the blessing of the mind (the mind pursuing, etc.) — nevertheless, he must not break through to Hashem. His entire <span class="h">tikun</span> is through the obstructer (= <span class="h">Keser</span>, <span class="v">"only the throne"</span>) — <span class="v">"I am Hashem; My holiness is above..."</span>; he must not break through. Earthly kingship mirrors heavenly kingship — as the Midrash states: we learn from Pharaoh regarding the awesome holiness of Hashem, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 41 — <i>Avraich</i>: The <i>Tikunim</i> Alluded to in Yosef's Title</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"They called before him: Avraich."</span> <span class="h">Avraich</span> alludes to the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>. <span class="h">Avraich</span> = "a father to the king" — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Malchus</span>, as above. <span class="h">Avraich</span> = knees [<span class="h">birkayim</span>] (as Rashi explains) — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the feet. <span class="h">Avraich</span> = blessing [<span class="h">berachah</span>] — the blessing of the hands. <span class="h">Avraich</span> = <span class="h">av</span> in wisdom, etc. — the blessing of intellect and wisdom. This is the aspect of <span class="h">Tzafnas Pa'anayach</span> — "revealer of hidden things." He merits the above nine chambers that do not cling and are not known — from which all awesome, concealed secrets and mysteries are perceived.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 42 — "Do Not Be Saddened": Gathering to the <i>Tzadik</i> = Joy</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Now, do not be saddened."</span> Since they merited gathering together to the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef), certainly there is no more cause for sadness — only great joy: <span class="mx">"when the righteous multiply, the people rejoice."</span> Whoever comes to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> within the gathering is in the category of a <span class="h">tzadik</span>: <span class="v">"your people are all righteous"</span> — with them is the source of joy: <span class="v">"rejoice, <i>tzadikim</i>, in Hashem"</span>; <span class="v">"sing joyfully, <i>tzadikim</i>, in Hashem."</span> But due to the bitterness of exile (general and personal), the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span> within each person is concealed. However, as soon as they gather to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> — <span class="v">"a man in whom there is spirit"</span> — the point of <span class="h">tzadik</span> in each one is illuminated. Through this, joy increases: <span class="mx">"the light of the righteous makes glad"</span> (as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Chadi Rabbi Shimon</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:61</span>, on this verse).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore then: <span class="v">"the spirit of Yaakov their father was revived"</span> — vitality and joy. As it says: <span class="v">"my son Yosef is yet alive — great!"</span> Rashi explains: <span class="mx">"great is my joy and gladness"</span> etc. The essential joy — truth and holy <span class="h">emunah</span> — all vitality and joy — comes essentially through the children one begets: through this Israel multiplies, the aspect of <span class="v">"a wise son gladdens a father"</span>; <span class="mx">"may the father rejoice in his offspring."</span> Holy <span class="h">emunah</span> must be revealed from generation to generation — this is the essential joy. <span class="v">"Serve Hashem with joy... know that Hashem is G-d"</span> — the essential joy — concluding: <span class="v">"from generation to generation is His faithfulness."</span> <span class="v">"I shall sing of Hashem's kindnesses forever; from generation to generation I shall make known Your faithfulness."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential joy in Him — begun by Avraham who first revealed <span class="h">emunah</span> in the world — was primarily revealed through Yaakov, whose bed was complete (twelve tribes, all <span class="h">tzadikim</span>). With each tribe born, joy increased. But the essential inflaming of joy's light was through Yosef's birth, as above. Yet the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> provoked greatly — creating dispute among them, a great flaw in joy. The essential joy is through love and peace: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out and in peace be led."</span> The essential thing: Israel gathering together to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef) — through which comes the essential joy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 43 — Binyamin's Portion: The <i>Bais HaMikdash</i> as the Place of Joy</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the climax came through Binyamin — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was in his portion, the holy, awesome Place where one can receive this holy light of joy. In truth, the essential completeness of joy is through all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span> — drawn through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> who gladdens Jewish souls, who sort from the Chambers of Exchange and ascend, etc. — the mind pursuing, the Arranger obstructing, etc. — the nine chambers fashioned through the pursuit and obstructer (= <span class="h">Keser</span>), where the source of joy resides: <span class="v">"eternal joy upon their heads."</span></div>

<div class="p">But this joy is extremely difficult to bear in this world — it is above <span class="h">nefesh, ruach, neshamah</span>, etc. One needs the awesome Place — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — deriving at its root from "that Place" (= <span class="h">Keser</span>, called Place): <span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place"</span>; <span class="v">"he encountered the place"</span> (Yaakov — ois kaf-daled above). This Place was Binyamin the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s portion. Though Yosef was born and could gladden Israel, the holy bed was not complete until Binyamin was born in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. No place in the world can bear this light of joy — rejoicing in Him, which is beyond expression: each person according to what he can measure in his heart. Only the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where the source of joy is: <span class="v">"the King has brought me into His chambers — we shall exult and rejoice in You."</span> From there joy flows to all Israel, each according to his good deeds — and essentially according to his closeness to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, who can gladden even the very worst, transforming sorrow and sighing to joy.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 44 — In Exile: The <i>Tzadik</i>'s <i>Da'as</i> Replaces the <i>Bais HaMikdash</i>; Ben Oni / Binyamin</div>

<div class="p">Now, in exile with the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> destroyed, joy is extremely difficult — except through the power of <span class="h">tzadikim</span> with great, wondrous <span class="h">da'as</span>, considered equivalent to the building of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>: <span class="mx">"whoever has <i>da'as</i>..."</span> etc. They draw upon us joy in Him so we can survive this bitter exile.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore his mother named him <span class="h">Ben Oni</span> [son of my mourning] — Rachel died then and was buried at the crossroads, so Israel could weep at her grave during the destruction, as our Sages say. Rachel, seeing the great destruction that would come before the final end, named him <span class="h">Ben Oni</span> — for the great mourning and weeping needed every midnight over the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. Every person must cry out and weep each day over his flaws that delay the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s rebuilding: <span class="v">"a voice is heard on high, lamentation, bitter weeping — Rachel weeps for her children."</span></div>

<div class="p">But his father called him <span class="h">Binyamin</span> — for <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>: Yaakov called him "son of the right" for the ultimate end, when we return to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> is built in Binyamin's portion — where the source of joy resides. Yaakov said: even now he should be called <span class="h">Binyamin</span> (= <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> = joy). Even now in exile, it is forbidden to be in sadness — one must cry out and pour out his heart for some time each day, but the rest of the day be in joy, gladdening his soul through all the counsel. Through joy comes the essential sorting from the Chambers of Exchange — through which one specifically leaves the exile, each according to his level: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 45 — The Goblet in Binyamin's Sack: Hidden Mercy in Bitter Sorrow</div>

<div class="p">This is the great bitterness when the goblet was found in Binyamin's sack — yet through this specifically, Yosef revealed himself and they were gathered together: <span class="v">"Yosef could not restrain himself"</span> — and joy increased, as above. This is a hint: when a person is in great, bitter distress that twists his heart — let him know and believe that this too is for good. Certainly wondrous mercy is hidden within the bitterness. He must only cry out and pray to Hashem and wait for His mercy. <span class="h">Gavi'a</span> [goblet] in <span class="h">gematria</span> equals <span class="h">yud-gimmel ayin-bais</span> [13 × 72] — the thirteen <span class="h">Mechilin d'Rachamay</span>, the totality of all mercy and great kindness. Specifically through the goblet in Binyamin's sack (which caused such bitter distress), they were gathered together and it was revealed that the goblet is wondrous mercy — thirteen times seventy-two: the thirteen <span class="h">Mechilin d'Rachamay</span>, the totality of all mercy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 46 — "I Rejoiced When They Said: Let Us Go to the House of Hashem"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"I rejoiced when they said to me: let us go to the house of Hashem."</span> The essential joy is in the house of Hashem — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, deriving from "the Place of the world": <span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place,"</span> as above. <span class="v">"Our feet were standing in your gates, Yerushalayim"</span> — there is the essential standing and sustenance of the feet. The essential thing is to sort the feet, as above — but their complete sorting is when one comes on foot to the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (and likewise to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, who are in the aspect of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>), where the essential completeness of joy resides.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Yerushalayim, built as a city joined together; there the tribes ascended, the tribes of G-d, a testimony to Israel, to thank the Name of Hashem."</span> Yerushalayim below corresponds to Yerushalayim above — the above supernal Place (<span class="v">"he encountered the place"</span>). There the tribes ascended — to thank [<span class="h">l'hodos</span>]: all the ascents through the above <span class="h">tikunim</span> are to ascend to the supernal Place, where the essential <span class="h">todah</span> and thanksgiving in joy resides — <span class="v">"to thank the Name of Hashem."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Pray for the peace of Yerushalayim... peace within your walls... for the sake of my brothers and friends, I shall speak of peace within you."</span> The essential thing depends on peace, as above. Dispute is hatred and anger — sadness from the Chambers of Exchange, which exchange and reverse everything, implanting false reasoning in the heart to hate one's fellow for nothing — even among <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and the upright, multiplying dispute through their reversed reasoning. This is the dispute of the tribes against Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, selling him as a slave. But in the end, everything is transformed for good through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who know how to draw joy even in the bitterness of exile, extracting from the exile and the Chambers of Exchange, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 47 — Yehudah and Yosef: <i>Mashiach ben David</i> and <i>Mashiach ben Yosef</i></div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"Yehudah approached him"</span> — the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> through Yehudah and Yosef uniting — the essence of <span class="h">Mashiach ben David</span> (from Yehudah) and <span class="h">Mashiach ben Yosef</span>. David is the aspect of <span class="h">Tehillim</span>, composed with the ten types of melody (= joy) — the sweet singer of Israel. The inflaming of joy in the heart (= Yosef, <span class="v">"the house of Yosef a flame"</span>) — its essential revelation is through words of thanksgiving and praise (= David, who satiated the Holy One with songs and praises). Speech has great power — through it the essential revelation of joy: <span class="v">"a man has joy in the answer of his mouth"</span>; <span class="v">"worry in a man's heart — let him quash it; a good word gladdens it."</span> This is: <span class="v">"lips of song shall my mouth praise"</span> specifically — the aspect of David from Yehudah, named for thanksgiving: <span class="v">"this time I shall thank Hashem."</span></div>

<div class="p">Yehudah said to Yosef: <span class="v">"let your servant speak a word in the ears of my lord."</span> Ears are the instrument of hearing; <span class="mx">"hearing depends on the heart"</span> — where the root of joy resides: <span class="v">"You placed joy in my heart."</span> This joy is aroused by Yosef, who inflames the heart by blowing away the dust. But the essential revelation and sustaining of joy is through speech (= David from Yehudah). Therefore Yehudah said to Yosef: <span class="v">"let your servant speak a word"</span> — specifically a word: holy words of the joy of holy <span class="h">emunah</span>, as we proclaim daily: <span class="mx">"fortunate are we — how good is our portion!"</span> etc. <span class="v">"In the ears of my lord"</span> — <span class="mx">"hearing depends on the heart."</span> Rashi: <span class="mx">"let my words enter your ears."</span> Through the speech, the words enter his ears and heart until joy shines in completeness and sadness (= anger and judgment) is nullified. <span class="v">"Let not your anger burn against your servant"</span> — anger nullified through the intensity of joy shining through speech, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 48 — Yaakov's Care That Binyamin Be Born in <i>Eretz Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore Yaakov was extremely careful that the holy bed not be completed outside <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, as stated in the holy Zohar — when Yosef was born he said: <span class="v">"send me away."</span> Had Binyamin been born outside <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, Yaakov could not have left, as the Zohar states. Binyamin — the completion of the holy bed, the ultimate completeness of joy — through him joy is drawn from "the Place of the world" to the place of this world. The essential holy Place is the awesome place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. Had he been born, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, in the impure place of Lavan's house outside <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, joy would have been drawn to the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — whose entire overpowering is through drawing joy to themselves: all the joys of worldly vanities (= the laughter of the fool): <span class="v">"of joy — what does it accomplish?"</span> We plead: <span class="v">"let not my enemies rejoice over me."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore he ensured the holy bed was completed through Binyamin's birth — so joy would be drawn to its proper place, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where holy joy in Him resides. From there we can draw holy joy even now in exile, outside <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. Wherever Israel is exiled, they draw the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> through <span class="h">shuls</span> and <span class="h">batay midrash</span> (= a miniature <span class="h">Mikdash</span>), and essentially through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 49 — David Found the Place: The <i>Ge'ulah</i> Through <i>Mashiach ben David</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential finding of that awesome Place was through David specifically, who labored and toiled exceedingly to find it: <span class="v">"remember, Hashem, for David all his affliction... if I enter my tent... if I give sleep to my eyes... until I find a place for Hashem."</span> He was the sweet singer of Israel, engaged in words of songs and praises always — through which the awesome Place is essentially revealed, where His G-dliness is revealed, which is all our joy. Therefore the essential <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> will be through <span class="h">Mashiach ben David</span>, encompassing both David and Yosef, as is known. Every Jew must draw upon himself at all times the holiness of joy in Him through holy words, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 50 — <i>Birchas HaNisim</i>: The Blessing over Miracles "In This Place"</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Birchas HaNisim</span> — the blessing recited when arriving at a place where a miracle was performed for oneself or for our forefathers, as stated in the Shulchan Aruch (siman 218): one who sees a place where miracles were performed for our forefathers blesses: <span class="mx">"blessed is He who performed miracles for our forefathers in this place."</span> When arriving at a place where a miracle was performed for oneself: <span class="mx">"blessed is He who performed a miracle for me in this place."</span> Our Sages specified that the place be mentioned in the blessing — <span class="v">"in this place"</span> specifically.</div>

<div class="p">All miracles derive from that Place — the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, whose awesome holiness derives from the above supernal Place (= <span class="h">Keser</span>): <span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place."</span> From the obstructer (called Place) many <span class="h">tzimtzumim</span> descended until this physical place came into being — the dust of the earth upon which we stand in this World of Action. Here resides all our free will — for which everything was created. For the sake of free will, the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> (= the Chambers of Exchange) clings to this physical place — wanting to exchange and reverse everything from good to evil, from truth to falsehood, etc. Man's essential service: to stand the test, not follow the counsel of the wicked who reverse everything through their reasoning, but cling to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and follow their counsel, until he merits his eternal purpose.</div>

<div class="p">The essential power of the Chambers of Exchange to mislead is according to the flaw in the Arranger and Settler of the mind (= the obstructer, the aspect of Place) — due to which one lacks the <span class="h">yishuv hada'as</span> to overpower properly, to look at the truth and walk the true path. This derives from not yet meriting the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the supernal Arranger and Settler. In the matter of the pursuit and obstructer — from which one perceives what one perceives — everything depends. Even awesome, great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> flawed in this by not properly rectifying the Arranger and Settler — pursuing excessively and breaking through to ascend to Hashem: the flaw of Nadav and Avihu, <span class="v">"when they drew near before Hashem and died,"</span> as above. The aspect of: <span class="mx">"he peeked and was stricken; he peeked and died."</span> This was the essential sin of Adam HaRishon, as the holy Zohar states: he gazed at what he had no permission to gaze at. He flawed greatly — following the serpent's counsel, drawn after the desire of eating from the Tree of Knowledge (where the Chambers of Exchange essentially cling: <span class="v">"the flaming, revolving sword"</span>). Not only that, they gazed above at what they had no permission: <span class="v">"the tree was good for eating, desirable to the eyes, and coveted to make wise"</span> — the serpent enticed them to pursue their desires and gaze at lofty wisdoms and perceptions they had no permission to gaze at, especially after such flaws. This is the flaw of all the philosophers and false-wisdoms of today — pursuing all desires (eating, drinking, immorality, money, honor, anger, contentiousness, etc.) yet wanting to understand, intellectualize, and gaze at His G-dliness and His ways of governance — until they went astray as they went astray. All this is the flaw of the Arranger and Settler of the mind. From this come all troubles and sufferings, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — the Chambers of Exchange overpowering, from which all dangers and troubles, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>.</div>

<div class="p">When Hashem has mercy and performs a miracle, the essential miracle derives — through the power of the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — from that Place where the above nine chambers are perceived (not clinging, not known, etc.) — above the intellect. From there come all miracles — which are above the intellect. Intellect dictates that things should follow nature (the sea remains sea, dry land remains dry land), but when Hashem performs a miracle — splitting the sea into dry land — this is above the intellect. Therefore the philosophers, who do not nullify their minds, do not believe in miracles, laboring to deny them, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — despite our having seen countless miracles and wonders with our own eyes.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore all miracles derive from the above nine chambers (from <span class="h">Keser</span>, the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger, etc. = the aspect of Place). These nine chambers are above the intellect — not clinging, not known. In truth, all miracles derive from the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where ten miracles occurred. The essential holiness of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> is the tenth holiness — <span class="h">Keser</span>, the aspect of Place — from which all miracles in the world derive. Therefore when Israel left Egypt with wondrous miracles and the sea was split, they sang: <span class="v">"You shall bring them and plant them on the mountain of Your inheritance"</span> — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, from which the essential miracles of the Exodus and the splitting of the sea derive. The essential purpose was to come there: <span class="v">"He brought us out from there to bring us to..."</span> etc., as explained elsewhere.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore when a miracle is performed, one must bless: <span class="v">"who performed a miracle for me in this place"</span> — mentioning the place specifically. The essential miracle in this place derives from the awesome holiness of the root of the Place — the above Arranger, from which the nine chambers (not clinging, etc.) derive — from which all miracles come, as above.</div>

